<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Internal rebellion</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Internal rebellion is a recurring pattern in which revolts by peasants, provinces, generals or religious movements weaken or overthrow a ruling power. Rebellions often arise from heavy taxation, famine, misrule or ethnic grievances. Examples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>, the Red Turban revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, and the uprisings that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>.</p></div>
